+# Classification Floor
+
+Status: approved
+Slug: classification-floor
+
+## Roles
+- implementer: builds the change described here
+- reviewer: checks the change against this spec
+- qa: checks the scenarios below with tests
+- product_owner: owns the purpose and accepts the result
+
+## Purpose
+Routing picks the reviewer panel from the work order's risk and surfaces, and intake, an agent station, used to overwrite both. One low rating from a model was enough to drop a full panel to two reviewers, and nobody decided it. This spec makes the classification a floor: intake may raise it, deterministic rules decide what is kept, and the content of the change can raise it again before any reviewer runs. A reclassification can no longer shrink the panel.
+
+## Requirements
+- The runner records the risk and surfaces as submitted, before intake runs. Intake output is a proposal, combined with the submitted values by classify_after_intake, a pure function.
+- Risk is a floor. A rated work order keeps the higher of its submitted risk and the intake proposal (low < medium < high). Intake can raise the risk, never lower it.
+- An unrated work order takes the intake rating, with one exception: a low rating is refused when the work order carries a risk hint the human gate scans for. The work order then stays unrated, which routes to the full panel.
+- Surfaces are a union. Intake can add a surface, never remove a submitted one.
+- After the builder runs, and only when a routing record exists, the runner derives surfaces from the builder's changed file paths with a fixed table (content_floor and surfaces_from_paths). Paths only are read, never file content or a model's description. A detected surface the work order did not declare is added to it.
+- When a low rated work order touches an undeclared sensitive surface (security, data, payments or deploy), its risk is raised to medium, so the reduced panel does not apply. Reviewers are routed again with the raised classification before any of them runs, and practices and skills are resolved again.
+- runs/<work-order-id>/routing.json records the classification (submitted values, intake proposal, and one sentence per decision) and every escalation (station, attempt, detected surfaces with evidence paths, reasons, panel before and panel after). The routing section of report.md lists the classification decisions and the escalation reasons. Both are omitted when empty.
+- A malformed intake proposal still fails the run as before: a risk that is not low, medium, high or null raises RiskValidationError, and surfaces that are not a list of known surfaces raise ValueError. The run is then recorded as aborted (see run-checkpoint).
+- The model policy ceiling and the routing table itself are unchanged.
+
+## Scenarios
+
+### Scenario: Intake cannot lower a submitted risk
+- S1: Given a work order submitted with risk medium, when intake proposes risk low, then the work order keeps risk medium, the full panel is selected, and the classification records that the proposal was not applied.
+
+### Scenario: Intake can raise the risk
+- S2: Given a work order submitted with risk low, when intake proposes risk high, then the work order risk becomes high and the classification records the raise.
+
+### Scenario: Surfaces are a union
+- S3: Given a work order submitted with the api surface, when intake proposes only the ui surface, then the work order carries both api and ui, and the classification records that the submitted surface was kept.
+
+### Scenario: A low rating is refused on an unrated work order with a risk hint
+- S4: Given an unrated work order that carries a risk hint the human gate scans for, when intake proposes risk low, then the work order stays unrated and the full panel is selected.
+
+### Scenario: An unrated work order otherwise takes the intake rating
+- S5: Given an unrated work order with no scanned risk hint, when intake proposes a risk level, then the work order takes that level and routing uses it.
+
+### Scenario: A sensitive change restores the full panel
+- S6: Given a low risk run whose panel was reduced at intake, when the builder's changed files signal an undeclared security, data, payments or deploy surface, then the risk is raised to medium and the full panel is selected before any reviewer runs.
+
+### Scenario: Changed UI files bring the design and UX reviewers
+- S7: Given a run whose work order did not declare the ui surface, when the builder changes UI files (for example .html or .css), then the ui surface is added and reviewer-design and reviewer-ux are selected when the pipeline includes them.
+
+### Scenario: Decisions and escalations are on record
+- S8: Given a run where intake's proposal was adjusted or the content of the change raised the classification, when the run reports, then routing.json carries the classification decisions and each escalation with the panel before and after, and report.md lists the decisions and the escalation reasons.
+
+### Scenario: A malformed proposal fails the run
+- S9: Given intake output with a risk outside the known levels or surfaces that are not a valid list, when the runner reads it, then the run fails with the validation error instead of ignoring the value.
