+# Clean Context Review
+
+Status: approved
+Slug: clean-review
+
+## Roles
+- implementer: builds the change described here
+- reviewer: checks the change against this spec
+- qa: checks the scenarios below with tests
+- product_owner: owns the purpose and accepts the result
+
+## Purpose
+Every reviewer in the panel receives the same packet: the work order, the upstream steps, the builder's report. They are independent of the builder but they share its framing, so an error in the spec or in the builder's account of what it did can pass all of them. This spec adds a reviewer that is given the change and nothing else, on a different model, and reasons backwards from the code.
+
+## Requirements
+- reviewer-clean is an agent station in the catalog. Its packet carries the station prompt, the work order id (needed to place the review file), the change under review and the output contract. It carries no title, description, risk hints, upstream steps, run context, builder report, learnings, practices, skills or design material.
+- The change under review is the diff against the base ref plus the content of untracked files when a git base ref is known, otherwise the content of the files the builder changed. Files under .openspec, brain, runs, reviews, work_orders and status are left out.
+- The change is cut at the packet token ceiling (max_packet_tokens, 12,000 by default). A cut is stated in the packet, with the number of files not shown, and the prompt tells the reviewer to say what it could not cover. With nothing changed the packet says so.
+- The station prompt tells the reviewer not to open the spec, the builder report or run output, to change no file, and to report only what it can point to in the change.
+- Routing treats it as part of the full panel: it runs for unrated, medium and high risk work and is routed out with the reduced panel on low risk. Whenever it is selected it runs on openai/gpt-6.1-sol, a model family other than the builder's.
+- Its review is written, validated and counted exactly like any other reviewer's.
+- It is opt in per pipeline. sdlc-default is unchanged; the sdlc-clean-review pipeline is sdlc-default with reviewer-clean added to the parallel panel.
+- Limit, stated plainly: the clean context is the packet and the instruction. The runtime still lets a reviewer read the repository, so the station cannot be technically prevented from opening the spec.
+
+## Scenarios
+
+### Scenario: The packet carries the change and none of the framing
+- S1: Given a run with reviewer-clean in the panel, when its packet is written, then it contains the changed code and the review file path, and does not contain the work order title or description, the upstream steps, the run context or the builder report.
+
+### Scenario: It runs on the full panel with a different model
+- S2: Given unrated, medium or high risk work, when reviewers are routed, then reviewer-clean is selected with the clean review model, which the runtime is handed; and given low risk work on the reduced panel, then it is routed out.
+
+### Scenario: Its review counts like any other
+- S3: Given a run where only reviewer-clean requests changes, when the approver aggregates, then the verdict is changes_requested.
+
+### Scenario: A large change is cut and says so
+- S4: Given a change larger than the packet ceiling, when the change under review is built, then it is cut, the number of files not shown is stated, and with no change at all the packet says none was found.
+
+### Scenario: The station and the pipeline are in the catalog
+- S5: Given the factory catalog, when it is loaded, then reviewer-clean is a station and sdlc-clean-review is sdlc-default plus reviewer-clean, while sdlc-default does not include it.
+
+### Scenario: With a git base ref the change is a diff
+- S6: Given a project in git with a base ref, when the change under review is built, then it shows the diff of tracked files and the content of untracked ones, and nothing from the spec.
