+# In-Run Loopback
+
+Status: approved
+Slug: in-run-loopback
+
+## Roles
+- implementer: builds the change described here
+- reviewer: checks the change against this spec
+- qa: checks the scenarios below with tests
+- product_owner: owns the purpose and accepts the result
+
+## Purpose
+When the checks gate or the approver sends work back, the runner recorded a loopback step and carried on to the end; the work only went back when someone started another run. This spec adds a second mode in which the runner itself sends the work back and runs the line again from there, inside the same run and the same budget.
+
+## Requirements
+- loopback_mode has two values. record, the default, is the earlier behavior: the loopback is recorded and the line carries on. in_run sends the work back and executes again from the station it returned to. The mode comes from the run_pipeline argument (anvil run --loopback in-run), else from loopback_mode in the base ref configuration, else record. Any other value raises ValueError.
+- in_run applies only to executed runs and only when the return station is in the pipeline. Packet mode never loops.
+- On an in-run loopback the runner drops the attempt's reviews, findings, gate failure and verdict, and continues from the return station (the builder, or spec-writer for a missing spec finding). The loopback step stays in the report with its reason.
+- The station the work returns to is told why: its packet gains a Loopback feedback section with the loopback reason and, for a gate failure, the gate output. The gate's loopback step carries the gate output as feedback.
+- max_loopbacks bounds in-run loopbacks exactly as it bounds recorded ones. The run thresholds (agent calls, run tokens, station caps) still apply to every call, so the budget can stop a looping run before the loopback limit does; the verdict is then budget_exceeded.
+- A write authority violation that was restored (see sealed-tests) does not follow the work into the next attempt: the approver judges the violations of the current attempt and any that could not be restored. Every violation stays in the report's integrity section.
+- The report records loopback_mode when it is in_run.
+
+## Scenarios
+
+### Scenario: Record mode is unchanged
+- S1: Given the default mode, when a reviewer requests changes, then the loopback is recorded, the builder is not called again, and the run ends with the failed verdict.
+
+### Scenario: In-run mode sends the work back and judges the new attempt
+- S2: Given in_run mode and a reviewer that objects to the first attempt and passes the second, when the run executes, then the builder and the reviewers run twice, only the second attempt's reviews are counted, and the verdict is approved with one loopback.
+
+### Scenario: The returning station receives the findings
+- S3: Given an in-run loopback to the builder, when the builder's second packet is written, then it carries a Loopback feedback section with the reason for the loopback.
+
+### Scenario: Loopbacks are bounded
+- S4: Given in_run mode and a reviewer that never passes, when the run executes, then the builder runs at most max_loopbacks times more, and the run ends with the failed verdict and a human required; and given the default call limit, then the budget stops the run first.
+
+### Scenario: A gate failure loops back in the run
+- S5: Given in_run mode and a builder that amends a sealed test on its first attempt and not on its second, when the run executes, then no reviewer runs on the failed attempt, the builder receives the gate output, and the second attempt can be approved.
+
+### Scenario: An unknown mode is rejected
+- S6: Given a loopback mode that is neither record nor in_run, when the run starts, then it raises a ValueError.
+
+### Scenario: The mode comes from the project configuration
+- S7: Given loopback_mode in_run in the project configuration, when a run executes with no argument, then loopbacks run in the run; and given an explicit argument, then the argument wins.
