+# Pre-Build Gate
+
+Status: approved
+Slug: pre-build-gate
+
+## Roles
+- implementer: builds the change described here
+- reviewer: checks the change against this spec
+- qa: checks the scenarios below with tests
+- product_owner: owns the purpose and accepts the result
+
+## Purpose
+The builder is the most expensive call in the line, and nothing checked the cheapest questions before paying for it: do the tests cover every scenario of the spec, and can this project verify its own work at all? This spec adds a deterministic gate in front of the builder that asks both, with no model call, and stops the run when the answer is no.
+
+## Requirements
+- The gate runs in executed runs immediately before the builder is called, on every attempt. It makes no model call.
+- Consistency: for every spec the run works from, every scenario id in the spec must appear in the project's tests (the existing tdd_gate). The specs the run works from are the ones spec-writer or blueprint wrote or changed in this run, plus the spec the work order names in spec_path.
+- Readiness: when the base ref configuration sets require_checks true and names no check command, the gate fails, because nothing could prove the work before review. new-project sets require_checks true for a blueprint that names checks.
+- A failed gate is recorded as a pre-build-gate step with its findings, the builder is not called, and the run ends with verdict gate_failed.
+- A passed gate is recorded as a pre-build-gate step before the builder. When there is nothing to check (no spec identified and checks not required) no step is added.
+- The spec-writer and blueprint steps record the spec slugs they wrote or changed (spec_slugs), so a resumed run checks the same specs.
+
+## Scenarios
+
+### Scenario: A scenario without a test stops the run before the builder
+- S1: Given a run where spec-writer added a scenario and the tests do not reference its id, when the line reaches the builder, then the builder is not called, a failed pre-build-gate step names the missing scenario, and the verdict is gate_failed.
+
+### Scenario: Every scenario in the tests lets the builder run
+- S2: Given a run where every scenario id of the changed spec appears in the tests, when the line reaches the builder, then a passed pre-build-gate step is recorded just before the builder step and the builder runs.
+
+### Scenario: A project that requires checks and has none does not build
+- S3: Given a configuration with require_checks true and no check command, when the line reaches the builder, then the gate fails, the builder is not called, and the step records that no checks are configured.
+
+### Scenario: Nothing to check adds no step
+- S4: Given a run whose spec was not changed, whose work order names no spec and whose project does not require checks, when the line reaches the builder, then no pre-build-gate step is recorded and the builder runs.
+
+### Scenario: The spec the work order names is checked
+- S5: Given a work order whose spec_path names a spec, when the line reaches the builder, then that spec's scenarios are checked against the tests even though spec-writer changed nothing.
