+# Run Checkpoint
+
+Status: approved
+Slug: run-checkpoint
+
+## Roles
+- implementer: builds the change described here
+- reviewer: checks the change against this spec
+- qa: checks the scenarios below with tests
+- product_owner: owns the purpose and accepts the result
+
+## Purpose
+The run report was written once, at the end of the line. A run that was killed, crashed or cancelled before that point left its work on the branch and no accounting: no steps, no usage, no verdict, and a second paid run was needed only to produce the paperwork. This spec makes the report a by-product of running: the runner saves it after every station and on any exit, so a run that dies is itself a complete, recorded result.
+
+## Requirements
+- The runner writes runs/<work-order-id>/report.json after every station step, with run_state in_progress and every step recorded so far. While the line is running and no approver verdict exists, the saved verdict is incomplete.
+- Before an agent station is called, the runner saves the report with an in_flight entry naming the station and the time the call started, so a run killed during a call still names the station that was running.
+- run_pipeline catches any exit from the line, including KeyboardInterrupt and SystemExit, writes the report with run_state aborted and an abort_reason naming the exception, and re-raises the original exception. A failure while writing the aborted report never replaces the original exception.
+- The aborted report keeps every completed step. When a station call was in flight, it adds that station as a failed step marked aborted, with usage_complete false. If the station's usage file (<station>.output.md.usage.json, schema anvil/usage@1) exists, the step carries the usage and attempts it had reported so far; otherwise the step has no usage and counts as an unmeasured call.
+- A run that reaches the end of the line records run_state completed. The report always carries run_state; abort_reason and in_flight appear only when set.
+- report.json is written through a temporary file that then replaces it, so a reader never sees a partial report. A checkpoint that cannot be written adds one report warning and never stops the line.
+- The run command installs a SIGTERM handler that exits with code 143, so a stop request (for example a cancelled workflow) becomes an exit the runner catches and records. A process killed without a catchable signal keeps the last checkpoint.
+- Resuming is a separate capability (see run-resume). Without it, a new run of the same work order starts from the first station.
+
+## Scenarios
+
+### Scenario: The report is saved after every station
+- S1: Given a run in execute mode, when a station step completes, then report.json exists with run_state in_progress and that step in it; and when an agent station is about to be called, then the saved report names it under in_flight.
+
+### Scenario: A crashed or interrupted run leaves an aborted report
+- S2: Given a run whose executor raises an unexpected exception or is interrupted, when run_pipeline exits, then report.json records run_state aborted, an abort_reason naming the exception, and every step that completed before it, and the exception is re-raised to the caller.
+
+### Scenario: The interrupted station is recorded with the usage it had reported
+- S3: Given a run interrupted while a station call was in flight, when the aborted report is written, then it contains that station as a failed step marked aborted, with the usage from its usage file when one exists, and with no usage (an unmeasured call) when none exists.
+
+### Scenario: A finished run is recorded as completed
+- S4: Given a run that reaches the end of the line, when the final report is written, then run_state is completed and the report has no abort_reason and no in_flight entry.
+
+### Scenario: The report is written atomically
+- S5: Given a report being saved, when the write happens, then the content is written to a temporary file that replaces report.json, and no temporary file is left behind after a successful write.
+
+### Scenario: A stop request saves the report
+- S6: Given the anvil run command, when the process receives SIGTERM, then it exits through SystemExit with code 143, which the runner records as an aborted run before the process ends.
