+# Resume an Aborted Run
+
+Status: approved
+Slug: run-resume
+
+## Roles
+- implementer: builds the change described here
+- reviewer: checks the change against this spec
+- qa: checks the scenarios below with tests
+- product_owner: owns the purpose and accepts the result
+
+## Purpose
+The run checkpoint keeps the accounting of a run that dies, but the next run of the same work order started from the first station and paid again for everything that had already finished. This spec lets a run continue from where the aborted one stopped.
+
+## Requirements
+- anvil run --resume (run_pipeline resume=True) continues an earlier run of the same work order when its report.json records run_state aborted or in_progress. Resume applies to executed runs only.
+- The resume point is the first station in pipeline order that has no finished step in the earlier report. A step counts as finished when its status is executed, completed, passed, not_configured, routed_out, skipped or packet_ready and it is not the interrupted step. A reviewer step counts only when its review file is still on disk and parses as a countable review.
+- Steps before the resume point are carried into the new report marked carried true, with their usage, so run thresholds and totals count the earlier spend once. The interrupted step is dropped; that station runs again.
+- State is restored from the run record, not from a model: risk, surfaces and intake outcome from the saved work order, the routing record (panel, models, classification, escalations) from routing.json, the reviews of carried reviewers from their review files, the approver verdict and decision from the earlier report, and loopback count, warnings, evidence, deployment status and integrity violations from the earlier report.
+- The resumed report records resumed_from with the earlier run id, the station it resumed at and the number of carried steps. The earlier report is not archived to history, because its steps live on in the resumed report; archiving it too would count its usage twice.
+- When resume is requested and there is nothing to continue (no earlier report, a finished earlier run, or an abort before any station finished), the run starts from the first station, the earlier report is archived as usual, and a report warning says why.
+- Without resume, a rerun behaves as before: the earlier report, aborted or not, is archived and the line starts over.
+- Limits, stated plainly: the builder's file delta is not kept across processes, so a checks gate that runs after a carried builder judges the cumulative change against the base ref. Resume trusts the working tree as the aborted run left it.
+
+## Scenarios
+
+### Scenario: Resume continues from the interrupted station
+- S1: Given a run aborted while the builder was running, when the work order is run again with resume, then intake, spec-writer and test-writer are not called again, the builder and the stations after it run, and the run can end approved.
+
+### Scenario: Carried steps are marked and counted once
+- S2: Given a resumed run, when it reports, then the carried steps are marked carried, the report records resumed_from, and token usage counts each call of both runs exactly once.
+
+### Scenario: The aborted report is not archived
+- S3: Given a resumed run, when it finishes, then no copy of the aborted report is in the run's history and the status page totals count its usage once.
+
+### Scenario: Carried reviews are counted, and a missing one is run again
+- S4: Given a run aborted in the middle of the reviewer panel, when it is resumed, then the reviews already written are counted by the approver without calling those reviewers again; and given a carried review file that is gone, then that reviewer and the ones after it run again.
+
+### Scenario: Nothing to resume starts a normal run
+- S5: Given resume requested with no aborted run of the work order, or an abort before any station finished, when the run starts, then it runs from the first station and the report carries a warning saying so.
+
+### Scenario: The panel decided before the interruption is kept
+- S6: Given a run whose reviewer panel was reduced at intake and that was aborted later, when it is resumed, then the same reduced panel runs and the approver expects that panel.
+
+### Scenario: Resume is explicit
+- S7: Given an aborted run, when the work order is run again without resume, then the line starts from the first station and the aborted report is archived in history.
diff --git a/.openspec/specs/scaffold-checks/spec.md b/.openspec/specs/scaffold-checks/spec.md
