+++ b/.openspec/specs/scaffold-checks/spec.md
@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+# Checks and Protected Paths in New Projects
+
+Status: approved
+Slug: scaffold-checks
+
+## Roles
+- implementer: builds the change described here
+- reviewer: checks the change against this spec
+- qa: checks the scenarios below with tests
+- product_owner: owns the purpose and accepts the result
+
+## Purpose
+The checks gate stops reviewers from being spent on work that does not pass the project's own checks, but only when the project configures them. New projects were created with no checks and no protected paths, so the gate recorded itself as not configured on their first runs and review was launched on work nobody had verified. This spec makes a new project start with the gate's configuration in place.
+
+## Requirements
+- A blueprint may name checks: a list of non-empty command strings. A blueprint whose checks is not such a list is rejected when the catalog is loaded. The python-service blueprint names python3 -m unittest discover -s tests; the static-site blueprint names none.
+- new-project writes the blueprint's checks and the default protected paths (tests/, .openspec/, anvil.json, .anvil/) into anvil.json. When the blueprint names checks it also writes require_checks true, so the pre-build gate stops a run if every check command is later removed (see pre-build-gate).
+- init does not know the stack, so it writes an empty checks list and the default protected paths.
+- BLUEPRINT.md has a Checks section: it lists the commands the gate runs, or says that no check command is configured yet and where to add one.
+- onboard writes an empty checks list and the default protected paths into the anvil.json it creates for a brownfield project. It never overwrites an existing anvil.json.
+- A project with an empty checks list is still covered by the protected path rule (see write-authority).
+
+## Scenarios
+
+### Scenario: A python-service project starts with its checks
+- S1: Given new-project with the python-service blueprint, when the project is created, then anvil.json carries the blueprint's check command and the default protected paths.
+
+### Scenario: init writes protected paths and an empty checks list
+- S2: Given init on a new folder, when the project is created, then anvil.json carries an empty checks list and the default protected paths.
+
+### Scenario: A blueprint with malformed checks is rejected
+- S3: Given a blueprint whose checks is not a list of non-empty command strings, when the catalog is loaded, then it raises a ValueError naming the blueprint.
+
+### Scenario: BLUEPRINT.md states the checks
+- S4: Given a project created from a blueprint, when BLUEPRINT.md is read, then it lists the check commands the gate runs, or states that none is configured when the blueprint names none.
+
+### Scenario: onboard protects a brownfield project
