+# Station Token Caps
+
+Status: approved
+Slug: station-token-caps
+
+## Roles
+- implementer: builds the change described here
+- reviewer: checks the change against this spec
+- qa: checks the scenarios below with tests
+- product_owner: owns the purpose and accepts the result
+
+## Purpose
+The only token limit was the run threshold, and the allowance handed to each station was everything left in the run pool. One station could take the whole pool, and a correction retry was allowed until the pool was gone. This spec splits the pool: each station call gets its own cap, the smaller of that cap and what is left of the run, and an overrun is stated in the report.
+
+## Requirements
+- efficiency.max_station_tokens sets the token cap for one station call. efficiency.station_tokens.<station> sets a cap for one station and wins over the general cap. Both are positive integers; any other value is rejected when the configuration is read.
+- For a command runtime, the runner hands each agent station ANVIL_MAX_STATION_TOKENS equal to the smaller of its station cap and the remaining run pool (max_run_tokens minus the tokens used so far), never less than 1. With no station cap the handoff is the remaining run pool, as before. With neither a cap nor a run threshold the variable is not set.
+- The cap is recorded on the step as station_token_cap. When the call reports usage above the cap, the step carries station_cap_exceeded and the report gains a warning naming the station, the tokens used and the cap.
+- The honest limit: the cap is handed to the runtime and enforced between attempts (the wrappers refuse a correction attempt once the reported usage reaches it). A single running call cannot be cut off mid-flight, so one call can exceed its cap; the overrun is recorded, not prevented. The run thresholds are still checked between calls.
+- New projects get max_station_tokens 500000 in anvil.yaml. It is an adjustable starting setting, like the other efficiency values.
+
+## Scenarios
+
+### Scenario: The station receives the smaller of its cap and the remaining pool
+- S1: Given a station cap and a run threshold, when an agent station is called through a command runtime, then ANVIL_MAX_STATION_TOKENS is the smaller of the station cap and the tokens left in the run pool.
+
+### Scenario: A per-station cap overrides the general cap
+- S2: Given efficiency.max_station_tokens and an efficiency.station_tokens entry for one station, when that station is called, then its cap is the per-station value, and every other station uses the general cap.
+
+### Scenario: An overrun is recorded
+- S3: Given a station call that reports more tokens than its cap, when the step is recorded, then it carries station_token_cap and station_cap_exceeded, and the report has a warning naming the station, the usage and the cap.
+
+### Scenario: Invalid cap configuration is rejected
+- S4: Given a station cap that is zero, negative, a boolean or not an integer, or a station_tokens value that is not an object, when the efficiency configuration is read, then it raises a ValueError naming the field.
+
+### Scenario: New projects start with a station cap
+- S5: Given a project created by init or new-project, when its anvil.yaml is read, then efficiency.max_station_tokens is 500000.
+
+### Scenario: Without a cap the handoff is the remaining run pool
+- S6: Given a configuration with a run threshold and no station cap, when an agent station is called through a command runtime, then ANVIL_MAX_STATION_TOKENS is the remaining run pool, as before.
