@@ -0,0 +1,50 @@
+# Write Authority Over Protected Files
+
+Status: approved
+Slug: write-authority
+
+## Roles
+- implementer: builds the change described here
+- reviewer: checks the change against this spec
+- qa: checks the scenarios below with tests
+- product_owner: owns the purpose and accepts the result
+
+## Purpose
+The spec and the tests are what judge the work, and the rule that kept stations from editing them was mostly a prompt. The checks gate judged only the builder, and only against files that existed at the base ref, so a spec or test written earlier in the same run could be amended by the station it was meant to judge, and no other station was judged at all. This spec makes write authority a fact the runner measures: it hashes the protected files around every executed agent station, and a change by a station with no authority over the file rejects the run.
+
+## Requirements
+- Protected paths default to tests/, .openspec/, anvil.json and .anvil/ when the project configures none. protected_paths and write_owners are read from the base ref configuration, the same snapshot the checks gate uses, never from files a station changed during the run.
+- Write owners default to spec-writer and blueprint for .openspec/ and test-writer for tests/. A protected prefix with no owner may not be changed by any station during a run.
+- Around every agent station executed through the generic execution path, the runner hashes the content of every file under the protected prefixes (sha256), before and after. Files are compared by content, so a touch that changes nothing is not a change. Compiled Python files and __pycache__ are ignored.
+- A violation is a protected file that existed before the station ran and was modified or deleted by it, under a prefix the station does not own. New files are never violations. Files the same station created earlier in the run are its own work and are not judged.
+- The pre-review checks gate judges the builder against the base ref files and also against protected files written earlier in the same run (for example the test-writer's tests): modifying or deleting one fails the gate and returns the work to the builder.
+- The protected path rule needs no command, so it applies even when the project configures no checks. With no checks and no violation the gate still records itself as not configured.
+- When any violation was recorded in the run, the approver verdict is rejected regardless of the reviews. The rule sentence names the stations, the changes and the paths, and keeps what the review aggregation said.
+- The run report records an integrity section in execute mode (protected paths, write owners, violations). Each violating step lists its violations, its detail names them, and a report warning is added.
+- Limits, stated plainly: this is detection that fails the run closed, not a filesystem permission; the changed file is put back afterwards (see sealed-tests). The intake station is not hashed. A station that runs after the approver is recorded and warned about, but the verdict has already been given; deploy still requires an approved verdict.
+
+## Scenarios
+
+### Scenario: The builder cannot amend a test written earlier in the run
+- S1: Given a run where the test-writer wrote a test and the builder then modified or deleted it, when the pre-review gate runs, then the gate fails with a protected path finding naming the file, and no reviewer is spawned for that attempt.
+
+### Scenario: A station changing a protected file it does not own rejects the run
+- S2: Given a run where a station that does not own the prefix (for example a reviewer) modified an existing spec, when the approver aggregates, then the verdict is rejected regardless of the reviews and the rule names the station and the file.
+
+### Scenario: Owners may write their prefix
+- S3: Given a run where the spec-writer changes an existing spec or the test-writer changes an existing test, when the run reports, then no violation is recorded for those changes.
+
+### Scenario: New files and a station's own files are not violations
+- S4: Given a station that adds a new file under a protected prefix, or later changes a file it created earlier in the same run, when its writes are judged, then no violation is recorded.
+
+### Scenario: The rule applies without checks
+- S5: Given a project with no checks configured, when the builder modifies an existing protected file, then the gate fails on the protected path rule; and given no violation, then the gate records itself as not configured.
+
+### Scenario: Defaults and configuration come from the base ref
+- S6: Given a project that configures no protected paths, when a run starts, then the protected paths are tests/, .openspec/, anvil.json and .anvil/; and given a project that configures protected_paths or write_owners, then the values from the base ref configuration are used.
+
+### Scenario: Violations are on record
+- S7: Given a run with a write authority violation, when the report is written, then it carries an integrity section with the protected paths, the write owners and the violations, and the violating step lists them.
+
+### Scenario: Files are compared by content
+- S8: Given a protected file whose modification time changes but whose content does not, when the station's writes are judged, then no violation is recorded; and given a content change, then one is.
