+# Clean Context Review
+
+## Purpose
+
+Every reviewer in the panel gets the same packet: the work order, the upstream steps, the builder's report. They did not build the code, but they share its framing, so a mistake in the spec or in the builder's account of its own work can pass all of them. `reviewer-clean` is given the change and nothing else, runs on a different model, and reasons backwards from the code.
+
+## What the packet contains
+
+| In the packet | Left out |
+|---|---|
+| The station prompt | Work order title, description and risk hints |
+| The work order id (to place the review file) | Upstream steps and run context |
+| The change under review | The builder report |
+| The output contract | Learnings, practices, skills and design material |
+
+The change under review is the diff against the base ref plus the content of untracked files when a git base ref is known; otherwise the content of the files the builder changed. Files under `.openspec/`, `brain/`, `runs/`, `reviews/`, `work_orders/` and `status/` are never shown.
+
+The change is cut at the packet ceiling (`efficiency.max_packet_tokens`, 12,000 tokens by default). A cut is stated in the packet with the number of files not shown, and the reviewer is told to say what it could not cover.
+
+## When it runs
+
+- It is part of the full panel: unrated, medium and high risk work. On low risk work it is routed out with the reduced panel.
+- It runs on `openai/gpt-6.1-sol`, a model family other than the builder's, whenever it is selected.
+- Its review is written, validated and counted like any other: one objection keeps the run from being approved.
+
+## Turning it on
+
+It is opt in per pipeline, because it adds one agent call to every full panel run. `sdlc-default` is unchanged. Use the `sdlc-clean-review` pipeline, which is `sdlc-default` plus `reviewer-clean` in the parallel panel, or add the station to the panel of your own pipeline:
+
+```bash
+python3 -m anvil.cli run . --pipeline pipelines/sdlc-clean-review.json --work-order work-order.json --execute
+```
+
+## Limit
+
+The clean context is the packet and the instruction. The runtime still lets a reviewer read the repository to check how changed code is used, so the station cannot be technically prevented from opening the spec. The prompt tells it not to.
+
+## Spec reference
+
+Full specification: `.openspec/specs/clean-review/spec.md`
