new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1bf2283
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/RUN-CHECKPOINT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@
+# Run Checkpoint
+
+## Purpose
+
+The run report is saved while the line runs, not only at the end. A run that is cancelled, crashes or is killed still leaves `runs/<work-order-id>/report.json` with every step that completed and the usage recorded so far. No second run is needed to produce the accounting.
+
+## When the report is saved
+
+| Moment | `run_state` | What is in the report |
+|---|---|---|
+| Before each agent station call | `in_progress` | Steps so far, plus `in_flight` naming the station and when its call started |
+| After every station step | `in_progress` | Steps so far, including the one that just finished |
+| The line reaches its end | `completed` | The full report, as before |
+| The run is interrupted or crashes | `aborted` | Steps so far, the interrupted station, and `abort_reason` |
+
+While the line is running and no approver verdict exists yet, the saved verdict is `incomplete`. A finished run with no approver verdict still records `packet_ready`.
+
+## An aborted run
+
+`run_pipeline` catches any exit from the line, including `KeyboardInterrupt` and `SystemExit`, writes the aborted report, and re-raises the original exception. The report then carries:
+
+- `run_state`: `aborted`
+- `abort_reason`: the exception type and message
+- `in_flight`: the station whose call was running, when there was one
+- every completed step, unchanged
+- one extra step for the interrupted station: status `failed`, `aborted: true`, `usage_complete: false`
+
+The interrupted step takes its usage from the station's usage file, `runs/<work-order-id>/<station>.output.md.usage.json`, when the wrapper had written one. When there is no usage file the step has no usage and counts as an unmeasured call, so the gap is visible in `token_usage` instead of silent.
+
+## Stop requests
+
+`anvil run` installs a SIGTERM handler that exits with code 143. A cancelled workflow or a `kill` therefore becomes an exit the runner catches and records as aborted. A process killed with a signal that cannot be caught (SIGKILL) keeps the last checkpoint, which is at most one station behind.
+
+## Atomic writes
+
+The report is written to `report.json.tmp` and then moved over `report.json`, so a reader never sees half a report. A checkpoint that cannot be written adds one warning to the report and does not stop the line.
+
+## Resuming an aborted run
+
+```bash
+python3 -m anvil.cli run . --pipeline pipelines/sdlc-default.json --work-order work-order.json --execute --resume
+```
+
+With `--resume`, a run whose report says `aborted` or `in_progress` continues from the first station that did not finish. The stations before it are not called again:
+
+- Their steps are carried into the new report, marked `carried: true`, with their usage. Thresholds and totals count the earlier spend once.
+- Risk, surfaces and the reviewer panel come back from the saved work order and `routing.json`. Reviews already written are read back from their review files and counted; a carried reviewer whose review file is gone runs again.
+- The report records `resumed_from`: the earlier run id, the station it resumed at, and how many steps were carried.
+- The aborted report is not copied to `history/`, because its steps live on in the resumed report.
+
+When there is nothing to continue (no aborted run, or an abort before any station finished) the line starts from the first station and a warning says so. Without `--resume`, a rerun starts over and the earlier report is kept under `runs/<work-order-id>/history/` as before.
+
+Two limits: the builder's file delta is not kept across processes, so a checks gate that runs after a carried builder judges the cumulative change against the base ref; and resume trusts the working tree as the aborted run left it.
+
+## Spec reference
+
+Full specifications: `.openspec/specs/run-checkpoint/spec.md` and `.openspec/specs/run-resume/spec.md`
