+# Write Authority
+
+## Purpose
+
+The spec and the tests judge the work, so the work under review may not change them. Telling a station not to touch them is a prompt, and a prompt can be disobeyed. The runner therefore measures it: it hashes the protected files before and after every executed agent station, and a change by a station with no authority over the file rejects the run.
+
+## Protected paths and owners
+
+When a project configures none, the protected paths are:
+
+- `tests/`
+- `.openspec/`
+- `anvil.json`
+- `.anvil/`
+
+Each protected prefix can have owners, the only stations allowed to change existing files under it:
+
+| Prefix | Owners |
+|---|---|
+| `.openspec/` | spec-writer, blueprint |
+| `tests/` | test-writer |
+| any other protected prefix | none: no station may change existing files |
+
+A project can set both in `anvil.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "protected_paths": ["tests/", ".openspec/", "anvil.json", ".anvil/", "pipelines/"],
+  "write_owners": {
+    ".openspec/": ["spec-writer", "blueprint"],
+    "tests/": ["test-writer"]
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Both are read from the base ref configuration, like the check commands, so a run cannot widen its own authority.
+
+## What counts as a violation
+
+A violation is a protected file that existed before the station ran and was modified or deleted by it, under a prefix the station does not own.
+
+Not violations:
+
+- a new file under a protected prefix (the builder may add tests)
+- a file the same station created earlier in the run
+- compiled Python files and `__pycache__`
+
+Files are compared by content hash (sha256). A touch that changes nothing is not a change, and an edit that keeps the modification time is still one.
+
+## What happens
+
+1. **Pre-review gate, builder only.** The checks gate judges the builder against the base ref files and against protected files written earlier in the same run, such as the tests the test-writer just wrote. A violation fails the gate, no reviewer is spawned, and the work returns to the builder. This applies even when the project has no check commands.
+2. **Approver, every station.** If any station recorded a violation, the verdict is `rejected` regardless of the reviews. The rule sentence names the station, the change and the path, and keeps what the review aggregation said.
+3. **Report.** `report.json` has an `integrity` section (protected paths, write owners, violations). The violating step lists `integrity_violations`, and a warning is added.
+
+## Sealed files: the change is undone
+
+Before each station runs, the runner keeps a copy of every protected file (up to 2,000,000 bytes each), held by the runner process where no station can reach it. When a station changes one without authority, the runner writes the sealed copy back, and recreates a deleted file, before anything else runs. For the builder that is before the checks gate runs its commands, so the checks always run against the tests as the test-writer wrote them.
+
+Restoring does not forgive. The violation is recorded with `restored: true`, the gate still fails, and the approver still rejects the attempt. A file over the size limit is judged by hash but not kept, so its violation reads `restored: false`. `"restore_protected": false` in `anvil.json` switches restoring off.
+
+## Limits
+
+- This is detection that fails the run closed, then puts the file back. It is not a filesystem permission: the station can still write the file while it runs.
+- New files are never violations, so a station can still add a file that changes how existing tests behave (a test helper, for example). That is left to reviewer-tests.
+- The intake station is not hashed.
+- A station that runs after the approver (deployer, observer) is recorded and warned about, but the verdict was already given. Deploy still requires an approved verdict.
+- Only executed runs are judged. Packet mode runs no station, so there is nothing to measure.
+
+## Spec reference
+
+Full specifications: `.openspec/specs/write-authority/spec.md` and `.openspec/specs/sealed-tests/spec.md`. The gate side is in [CHECKS-GATE.md](CHECKS-GATE.md).
